[PASSAGE]
Every collection of proverbs is a collection of contradictions, and the collectors have always known it. Look before you leap; he who hesitates is lost. Many hands make light work; too many cooks spoil the broth. Absence makes the heart grow fonder, and out of sight, out of mind. Two responses have been available for as long as the collections have. The debunker concludes that folk wisdom is not wisdom at all but a heap, from which anything and its opposite can be drawn. The apologist replies that each saying is true within its domain, and that the wisdom lies in knowing which one applies.
The apologist's reply does less work than it appears to. Grant that each proverb carries a silent condition — look before you leap when the risk is unbounded and irreversible. The condition is not in the proverb. It is supplied by whoever quotes it, and supplying it requires having already judged the case. No proverb tells you which proverb applies, and there is no proverb about that, either. Whatever guidance is being provided, the proverb is not the thing providing it.
Watch how they are actually used. Nobody consults a proverb in advance, the way one consults a timetable. The saying arrives afterwards: when the cautious man has already declined the risk and wants his caution ratified, when the neighbour at the funeral must say something and cannot say nothing. It closes deliberation rather than informing it, and it closes it by lending a private decision the authority of nobody in particular — the anonymous, unanswerable dead. On this description the contradictory twin is not a defect in the corpus. The corpus has to stock both verdicts, or it could not ratify either.
Which means that asking whether folk wisdom is true has been asking of it something it never held out. "Many hands make light work," said to a man struggling with a table, is not a hypothesis about the productivity of labour; it is a summons, and summonses are not false. The question does not get answered. It stops being a question — and it should be said plainly what that costs, because the cost is not small. When the saying is cruel — the nail that stands up gets hammered down, delivered to a child who has been singled out — the natural reply is that this is simply not true, and that reply is exactly what the analysis has taken away. What remains is to contest the move: to ask who is being ratified, and against whom, and by what authority a dead consensus speaks. That is slower, it sounds like evasion, and it forfeits the great advantage the other side enjoys.
The advantage is that they were only quoting. The child, meanwhile, has to answer in the form the saying was delivered, and has nothing to hand but another saying.

[QUESTIONS]

Q1. The passage's central claim is that:
(A) folk wisdom is incoherent, since for every proverb the corpus supplies its opposite.
(B) proverbs are true only within domains, and the skill lies in identifying the domain correctly.
(C) proverbs are moves that ratify decisions rather than claims submitted for belief, so the question of their truth lapses — at a cost.
(D) the study of proverbs shows that ordinary speakers rely on inherited formulas in place of reasoning.

Q2. The author's attitude toward the debunker is best described as:
(A) partly agreeing with the debunker's premise while rejecting the verdict, since incoherence in the corpus is a condition of its working rather than a failure.
(B) fully endorsing the debunker, whose conclusion the third paragraph confirms.
(C) hostile, on the grounds that the debunker has confused a proverb's wording with its meaning.
(D) neutral, since the passage treats the debunker and the apologist as equally mistaken about equally unimportant matters.

Q3. The second paragraph contributes to the argument by:
(A) conceding a point to the apologist that the rest of the passage will quietly rely on.
(B) demonstrating that proverbs are false, since their silent conditions are almost never satisfied.
(C) proving that the debunker was right and that the corpus is a heap.
(D) showing that the judgment credited to the proverb is in fact made before the proverb is uttered, which prepares the shift to how proverbs are used.

Q4. Which case would the author's account handle most naturally?
(A) A manager cites "too many cooks" after deciding to cut a team, and cites "many hands" a month later when defending a hire.
(B) A student reads a book of maxims each morning and reports that his decisions have improved.
(C) A researcher tests "the early bird catches the worm" against employment data and finds no effect.
(D) A translator finds that a Japanese proverb has no equivalent in French and must be glossed.

Q5. If the passage's fourth paragraph did not name the cost of its own conclusion, the argument would:
(A) still stand, but as an analysis that quietly disarms its reader while presenting itself as neutral description.
(B) collapse, since the cost is the evidence on which the account of proverb-use rests.
(C) become stronger, since the concession is the only point at which the debunker's view resurfaces.
(D) be unchanged, the paragraph being an illustration of a claim already established.

Q6. The phrase "the anonymous, unanswerable dead" is used to indicate that a proverb:
(A) preserves the accumulated experience of earlier generations in compressed form.
(B) is felt to be older than it usually is, which is why speakers are so often mistaken about its origin.
(C) survives because no one alive has an interest in disputing it.
(D) confers on a private decision an authority with no author, and therefore no one to argue with.

[ANSWER KEY & ELIMINATION LOGIC]

Q1 — Correct answer: (C)
(C) CORRECT — Para 3 ("closes deliberation rather than informing it"; the corpus "has to stock both verdicts") plus para 4's dissolution and its named price.
(A) adjacent_answer — The debunker's position, reported in para 1; the author accepts the observation but denies the verdict, since para 3 makes contradiction functional.
(B) adjacent_answer — The apologist's position, dismantled in para 2: the condition is supplied by the quoter, not the proverb.
(D) framework_import — Substitutes a general thesis about formulaic thinking for the author's specific claim about what kind of speech act a proverb is.

Q2 — Correct answer: (A)
(A) CORRECT — Para 1's contradictions are granted; para 3 turns them into a requirement of the practice ("could not ratify either"), so the premise survives and the conclusion does not.
(B) stance_misread — Para 3 explicitly denies that the twin is "a defect in the corpus," which is the debunker's whole conclusion.
(C) straw_version — The debunker is never accused of confusing wording with meaning; his error is assuming proverbs were offering claims.
(D) false_symmetry — The two are not treated alike: the apologist is refuted in para 2, the debunker is partly absorbed in para 3.

Q3 — Correct answer: (D)
(D) CORRECT — "Supplying it requires having already judged the case" removes the proverb from the causal work of decision, which is what licenses para 3's pivot to use.
(A) adjacent_answer — Para 2 grants the apologist's premise only to strip it; nothing from the apologist is relied on later.
(B) category_slip — Recasts a point about who supplies the condition as a claim about truth-values, the very framing para 4 abandons.
(C) premature_closure — Stops at the paragraph's apparent destination; para 3 immediately withholds the debunker's verdict.

Q4 — Correct answer: (B)
Correction: the correct answer is (A).
(A) CORRECT — Exactly the ratification pattern of para 3: the decision comes first, the saying arrives to authorise it, and the contradictory twin is available when the next decision runs the other way.
(B) level_confusion — Treats proverbs as advance guidance, the consultation model para 3 denies ("nobody consults a proverb in advance").
(C) category_slip — Tests a proverb as an empirical hypothesis, which para 4 says is asking of it something it never held out.
(D) adjacent_answer — A real fact about proverbs, but about translatability, on which the passage takes no position.

Q5 — Correct answer: (A)
(A) CORRECT — Para 4 concedes that the analysis removes the reply "this is simply not true"; without that concession, the reader is left disarmed and unwarned, which is why the author states the price rather than conceding it.
(B) nec_suff_swap — Inverts support and consequence: the cost follows from the account of use; the account does not rest on it.
(C) adjacent_answer — The debunker does not resurface at the cost; the cost concerns what the reader loses, not what the debunker gains.
(D) example_promotion — Demotes the passage's costed verdict to decoration; the closing paragraph turns on it.

Q6 — Correct answer: (D)
(D) CORRECT — The phrase caps "lending a private decision the authority of nobody in particular," and para 4's closing complaint is precisely that the speaker "was only quoting."
(A) phrase_misuse — Reads the phrase as praise of inherited experience, the apologist's picture, when it names an authority without an author.
(B) adjacent_answer — Makes a point about the dating of sayings; antiquity is not what the phrase is doing.
(C) nec_suff_swap — Offers a condition of survival where the passage names a source of authority in use.

[BLUEPRINT NOTE] Structure: a corpus whose incoherence turns out to be a functional requirement once its speech-act category is corrected | Posture: refusal_dissolved | Thesis visible: para 3 (fully by para 4) | Traps: premature_closure toward the debunker (para 2), category_slip toward truth-evaluation (para 4)
